The full numbered plain-text report is saved in [referee_report.txt](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dsr_2/referee_report.txt). Reproducible checks are in [audit_checks.py](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dsr_2/audit_checks.py), with [results](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dsr_2/audit_checks.json). Repository source files were read only.

1. Theorem SB: CORRECT WITH FIXES; the blanket derandomizer conclusion is OVERCLAIMED.

SB(a) is correct. Hitting every subset of cardinality ceil(delta·phi) requires at least phi−ceil(delta·phi)+1 endpoints. The adaptive argument applies to membership-query access to an otherwise arbitrary set.

The Bernstein proof in SB(b) is also correct. Writing v=delta·phi and l=ln(8phi), its choice s=sqrt(6vl) satisfies s≤v/2. The Bernstein exponent is at least 18l/7>2l. The union of 2(phi−1)+1 failure events has probability less than 1/(16phi). The complex error is bounded by sqrt(2)s, as claimed.

Two subsequent deductions need correction.

First, Parseval’s actual lower bound is

  beta(S) ≥ sqrt(|S|(phi−|S|)/(phi−1)).

The hypotheses allow |S| to range between delta·phi/2 and 3delta·phi/2. They do not imply |S|=delta·phi(1+o(1)). Even substituting the nominal density delta, the displayed upper bound gives a ratio sqrt(48/(1−delta))·sqrt(l), which can reach 8sqrt(l), rather than 7sqrt(l). Using the actual permitted density, 11sqrt(l) is a safe consequence of the printed estimates.

This identifies a gap in the claimed constant’s justification, not a counterexample to a sharper existence theorem. The factor 7 is eventually valid in the intended DSR regime, where delta=o(1) and delta·phi/ln(phi) tends to infinity. Add those assumptions or change the comparison.

Second, lines 150–151 require |S|≥delta·phi, whereas SB(b) only constructs an approximately sized set. Use a fixed-cardinality random subset with suitable concentration, or increase the Bernoulli mean and recompute constants.

The assertion that every listed derandomizer’s guarantee contains every density-delta accepting set is false:

- Nisan/INW/Nisan–Zuckerman permit arbitrary terminal predicates. SB(a) applies to a generator required to handle all such predicates, provided the reference walk is sufficiently mixed and the approximation error is small enough.
- The width-independent HPV and BHPP results impose a single accepting state. Their stated classes do not contain arbitrary terminal subsets. For HPV’s PRG, summing endpoint errors requires epsilon=O(1/phi). BHPP’s HSG is not an additive-error PRG, so that summation argument cannot simply be transferred to it. [HPV](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.ITCS.2021.7), [BHPP](https://williamhoza.com/research/papers/BHPP/).
- GKM fools Fourier shapes, including individual endpoint characters. An arbitrary endpoint indicator is generally a linear combination of shapes, requiring an algebra-norm/error calculation. SB(a) is not directly a lower bound for GKM’s stated class. [GKM](https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.04350).
- KNP’s main theorem concerns a fixed finite group, equivalently constant-width permutation programs. Its group-dependent constants cannot be suppressed when G grows with N. De gives more explicit group dependence. [KNP](https://iuuk.mff.cuni.cz/~koucky/papers/bwbp.pdf), [De](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/5959811/).
- AKS/IZ amplification retains an initial seed; the direct parameter substitution fails here. That is different from the claimed universal endpoint-support impossibility theorem.

The defensible conclusion is that these black-box applications do not prove DSR. “Generic derandomizers cannot work [proved]” is too strong.

2. Proposition RW: CORRECT WITH FIXES.

The unweighted positive-prime measure is legitimate. A bound on the symmetrized eigenvalue alone would not control the modulus of a complex character sum. However, JMV’s underlying weighted prime estimate and partial summation do give

  |sum_{p≤y} chi(p)| ≪ sqrt(y)·ln(Ny),

hence lambda ≪ ln(y)·ln(Ny)/sqrt(y). This includes induced characters. [JMV, proof of Theorem 1.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0811.0647).

The deliberately loose constant 10^6 is supportable as a derived bound. It should not be attributed directly to JMV. The character-level explicit formula of Ernvall-Hytönen–Palojärvi supports a coarse psi bound of 10^4sqrt(x)ln³(Nx); allowing 2·10^4 after prime powers and partial summation still stays below 10^6. Crucially, use their character-level estimates, not their final AP theorem with x≥q, since here y=N^{o(1)}. [Explicit estimates](https://arxiv.org/html/2003.01925).

The Fourier/Parseval error bound is correct. A sufficient hitting length is

  t ≥ ln(4phi(1−delta)/delta)/(2ln(1/lambda)).

The printed formula drops 1−delta and is safely larger. The pointwise length is correct too.

Necessary qualifications:

- These finite lengths require lambda<1. Small y can give lambda=1.
- The stated hitting-length asymptotic requires delta=N^{-o(1)}, as for S_can. It is false for arbitrary S, for example delta=1/phi.
- The product height is N^{1+o(1)} at t_hit, but N^{2+o(1)} at t_infinity. Line 114 should distinguish them.
- Exactly log₂pi(B) fair bits per independent prime choice is not a literal fixed-bit implementation. A power-of-two prefix of the primes repairs this without changing the leading asymptotics.
- An implementation must replace unknown phi and lambda by certified bounds.

The output bounds and expected per-target cost L^{b+1/(2b)+o(1)} are correct. These are sufficient walk lengths and algorithmic upper bounds, not lower bounds on arithmetic-specific constructors.

3. Proposition AA: CORRECT; “sharpness” needs qualification; §2.7 is OVERCLAIMED.

The Fourier identity and normalization are correct:

  sum_a |h(a)−delta m|²
    ≤ lambda^{2k}m²phi·delta(1−delta).

Dividing by (delta m)² proves the stated exceptional-set bound.

The BOX lower bound must concern unit residues. After excluding nonunits, there are still (1−o(1))phi/B^k small nonsmooth residues, for fixed k and y=B. Thus the comparison with AA’s upper bound has the stated L^{1/(2b)+o(1)} gap.

That is a coarse comparison of bounds. It does not establish an asymptotic for |E| or prove that the reciprocal-density loss is optimal. “Sharp” is particularly uninformative when AA’s upper bound exceeds phi.

Enumeration contains pi(B)^k products, but testing/factoring every endpoint by trial division adds an L^{b+o(1)} factor unless a batching method is supplied. Distinguish enumeration size from total constructor cost.

The q-moment calculation is essentially correct. With d=pi(B), e=|Ghat[H]|, it gives

  sum_q |h(q^H)−delta m|²
    ≤ lambda^{2k}m²[
        de·delta(1−delta)
        + Csqrt(B)log²(NB)·A′(S)²].

Using only A′(S)²≤delta(1−delta)phi leads to the quoted N^{1+o(1)} enumeration scale for this template. It is a limitation of that upper-bound method, not a necessary condition for other second moments or algorithms.

The exceptional-set bullet has a substantive quantifier error: |E|<|T| proves that at least one target succeeds. DSR requires T∩E to be empty. Cardinality information alone guarantees that only when |E|<1.

For an orbit, J must count distinct residues or multiplicities must be retained. The global AA bound can prove one hit among sufficiently many distinct orbit points; it is not automatically a Parseval identity over a short orbit.

Finally, q↦q^H need not be injective. A B/polylog(N) lower bound on distinct targets can be obtained under GRH through collision probabilities, but must be justified rather than assumed.

4. BOX: CORRECT. BOX-infinity: CORRECT WITH FIXES/clarifications. Its refutation of dsr_1 §6 is CORRECT.

BOX(ii)’s uniqueness argument works. The vector (rw,1) is primitive. An independent vector no longer than it would give a nonzero determinant smaller than N; a dependent integral vector must be an integral multiple. This also handles ties.

The unconditional existence proof survives scrutiny:

- Distinguish B_*=floor(L_{2^h}[1/2,b]) from the actual B_N. The proposed z exceeds every relevant N-dependent preprocessing cutoff. Nonsmoothness at the larger cutoff implies nonsmoothness at the smaller one.
- For the translated interval, the divisibility errors are O(1), uniformly in its location. Bounded sieve weights at D=R^{1/2} give an O(D) remainder. Since s=ln D/ln z=u_h/2+o(1) tends to infinity, the fundamental lemma yields the required asymptotic. No distribution hypothesis at scale 2^h is being smuggled in. A suitable formulation is in [Tao’s sieve notes](https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2015/01/29/254a-supplement-5-the-linear-sieve-and-chens-theorem-optional/).
- Brun–Titchmarsh supplies the upper bound 2R/ln R in an arbitrary interval of length R. It does not require R to be a fixed power of N.
- There is at most one integer jth power in this interval for each j≥2, eventually, because its length is 2^{o(h)} while the relevant spacing is at least a constant times 2^{h/2}. Removing O(h) prime powers is harmless.
- The uniform smooth-count bound Psi(R,B_*)≪Rexp(−u_h/2) is valid. The chosen u_h makes this negligible relative to R/ln B_*. A bare Rankin Euler-product estimate retaining an extra log B factor would not suffice; cite the actual uniform bound or use smooth-number asymptotics. [A precise recorded reference](https://www.pollack-math.net/lambdadist3.pdf).

The remaining N is admissible, lies strictly between 2^{h−1} and 2^h, and has H=8h. Eventually r^8<N, giving the desired least residue. The canonical and Gauss multiplier ranges then follow.

For every fixed k, r^8B_N^k<N eventually. Therefore the correlation sum proposed in dsr_1 §6 is zero at q=2, while its asserted lower bound is positive. This genuinely refutes that lemma, including k=k(b) fixed independently of N.

It does not refute DSR. DSR permits signed representatives, nonzero lifts, |u| and |v| up to N³, and a separate w of potentially enormous height. BOX constrains the short positive combined multiplier with the specified canonical/Gauss test. A large w, or a lifted representative, escapes that argument.

The particular DD1 construction in DZ asks for the q=2 row. Deterministic factoring or a weaker PIN design need not literally construct that row. Orbit wrapping after O(log N) steps is possible; wrapping itself does not prove a hit.

5. Theorem FN: CORRECT WITH FIXES. Its broader certificate and novelty rhetoric is OVERCLAIMED.

The Fourier identity and normalization are correct. An empty translate gives

  delta ≤ lambda^k A′(S).

There is a genuine gap in choosing ell as the least prime above B: ell need not be a unit. For example, at b=0.8,

  N = 9250794819020799837774052793
    = 489733·18889465931478580854821,

has floor(L_N[1/2,0.8])=489711 and 3ln²N≈12439.96. The least prime above B is 489733, which divides N. The scratch checks include an exact primality certificate for the larger factor.

Fix this by choosing a prime ell∈(B,2B] not dividing N. There are far more primes in that interval than distinct prime divisors of N, eventually. All subsequent estimates survive.

The choices of k are otherwise valid, with an optional subtraction of one to eliminate endpoint discussion. The raised logarithmic factors do not destroy the powers of N:

  ln(lambda^{-k})
    ≥ k ln B/2 − O(k ln ln N)
    ≥ theta ln N − O_b(sqrt(ln N ln ln N)),

where theta=1/2 or 1/4. Thus the claimed N-exponents survive. The loss is generally at L scale, however, and must not be treated as L^{o(1)} in finer calculations.

The canonical density is correct. The coprime smooth-pair estimate needs proof or citation. A weaker density bound sufficient for FN is elementary: construct u and v from disjoint prime intervals inside (B/2,B], using t≈ln(sqrt N)/ln B distinct primes each. Each family has N^{1/2−o(1)} products; the pairs are coprime, short, and give distinct ratios. Hence delta_Gauss≥N^{-o(1)}.

The minorant extension is correct, including for real minorants that are negative outside S. On the missing translate their expectation is ≤0, so

  A′(f) ≥ (delta/2)lambda^{-k}.

Line 290 saying minorants remain open contradicts §2.4(i).

The canonical lower bound is sharp only up to N^{o(1)}. It does not establish random-set constants or norm asymptotics. The Gauss lower bound is not shown sharp.

After repair, FN does settle zroute’s stated speculative small-algebra-norm conjecture for the canonical shortest-vector set, under GRH. It excludes the specified norm-based certificate and dense minorants on those sets. It does not exclude every Bach-inspired arithmetic argument or alternative lifted test.

Its mechanism is elementary empty-translate duality plus the established GRH prime bound. The exact statement’s priority is unverified; missing relevant literature is discussed in item 10.

6. Lemma PT: CORRECT WITH FIXES to complexity; factoring-separation language is OVERCLAIMED. EQ-dom: CORRECT WITH FIXES to wording.

The choice of r establishes admissibility. For fixed 0<alpha<1, r=p^{o(1)}<p eventually, so N=pr has two distinct prime factors and satisfies the stated cutoffs. The H′ range and p^{3+o(1)} output heights follow.

Define the transferred cutoff explicitly as B_N. The argument does not promise the exact cutoff L_p[alpha,b], only constant b+o(1).

The claimed L_p[alpha,c+o(1)] running time does not follow:

- Finding r has an additional L-scale cost depending on b and C. A straightforward sieve up to its proposed threshold costs L_p[alpha,2^alpha max(b,C)+o(1)].
- A single DSR call costs L_p[alpha,c+o(1)].
- Producing outputs for all q≤B_N requires pi(B_N) calls, giving constant b+c, in addition to preprocessing.

State a per-input-q transfer with preprocessing separated, or the correct all-q total.

The Pollard–Strassen exponent is wrong. Searching for factors ≤X costs X^{1/2+o(1)}, up to log-size factors. Substituting X=N^epsilon gives N^{epsilon/2+o(1)}, not N^{epsilon/4+o(1)}. The familiar N^{1/4} bound results from searching to sqrt N. [Relevant discussion](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40993-022-00387-w).

PT is not a separation from deterministic factoring. It shows that DSR must work on a family whose factorization is already supplied. It does not prove that factoring-based methods cannot contain additional useful arithmetic ideas.

EQ-dom’s complexity-class equivalence works. For its forward direction, simply output phi(N)e_q after factoring N. The reverse follows from dominance, an affordable determinant, and ERH Miller splitting. Calling dominant rows for every factor-base prime “minimal” is inaccurate: the small-generator PIN condition is weaker.

7. Proposition SC: WRONG as an exact equivalence.

The forward greedy argument works. The reverse only gives u<BN³ for integer B, or the immediate bound ceil(B)N³ for real B.

There is an admissible counterexample:

  N=1065023=1031·1033, B=2, a=689969,
  d=2^40=1099511627776≤N²,
  c=2^21=2097152.

The least residue of a·d^{-1} is 1032129, and c=1032129+N, so j=1<B. The right-hand condition holds.

But

  dc=2^61=2305843009213693952
     > N³=1208027888215167167.

Every positive 2-smooth integer ≤N³ is 2^i with i≤60, and none is congruent to a. Indeed ord_N(2)=132870 and a=2^61 mod N.

This refutes the unconditional algebraic proposition as stated. It is not an asymptotic counterexample to GRH existence.

Exact fix: add

  d·(least_residue(a d^{-1})+jN)≤N³

on the right. Alternatively admit a ceil(B) height slack, obtaining N^{3+o(1)}, which remains different from literal DSR.

Also distinguish the number of smooth integer labels d≤N² from the number of distinct inverse residues, which is at most phi(N). A lifted-test density bound needs its own smooth-count estimate; “≤Bdelta” is not established as an exact inequality by the argument given.

SC reformulates a search. It does not establish a complexity equivalence proving “no gain,” because the height constraint couples d and the lift, and a constructor could exploit that coupling.

The distinction from Theorem J is correctly drawn: an average fixed-parameter Johnson-class result does not rule out worst-case arithmetic construction. The NP-hardness discussion needs its GRH-totality and deterministic-reduction qualifications; explicit-prime-list randomized hardness is a different statement.

8. Proposition IWD: CORRECT WITH FIXES. The categorical nonimplication claim is OVERCLAIMED.

One-trial success ≥delta/2 and ceil(8/delta) repetitions give failure probability ≤e^{-4}<0.02. The bounded computation has circuit size T=L^{O(1)}. Under the stated all-large-lengths E circuit-hardness hypothesis, IW supplies an O(log T)-seed generator; error <1/2 preserves positive success probability. Enumeration costs T^{O(1)}. The conditional DSR and factoring implications follow. [IW](https://www.math.ias.edu/~avi/PUBLICATIONS/MYPAPERS/IW97/proc.pdf).

For implementation, use a valid explicit density lower bound in the repetition count and charge prime-table generation, trial division, and generator evaluation in T. The power-of-two prime prefix has the requisite asymptotic spectral bound.

“No converse or complexity barrier is known” is defensible. “It would not imply circuit lower bounds because DSR is one explicit problem” is not a proof: one explicit problem can encode a rich family. Replace the categorical assertion by the knowledge claim.

9. Overall verdict: DSR remains open here—CORRECT. “Exact weakest obstacle,” probability typography, and several experiment interpretations—OVERCLAIMED.

TH-wrap is not established as equivalent to DSR or as the weakest sufficient hypothesis for factoring. EQ-dom itself supplies an alternative certificate task without the prescribed hitting format.

TH-wrap also needs a precise definition:

- Reduce numerator and denominator before defining multiplier height; otherwise padding by t·t^{-1} changes the alleged height without changing the residue.
- Specify how clearing inverse multipliers preserves |u|,|v|≤N³ and w’s Omega budget.
- Specify the budget on the combined multiplier. DSR generally only bounds Omega(vw) by 4log₂N, not the one-log bound printed for m.
- Define the tests and require them to return legal final witnesses or affordable dominant rows.

BOX only forces the stated alternative for the restricted canonical positive test. It does not force every DSR constructor into TH-wrap.

The ≤3% assessment is subjective, as disclosed. Neither PT nor BOX supplies mathematical calibration for that number or proves a strict hardness comparison with factoring.

E1’s P1a genuinely failed. The post-hoc observations do not establish that reduced L1 norms are merely a finite-size artifact. A lambda of 0.57 is compatible with a good expander. Mean real parts 0.33–0.86 do not mean near 1 on every prime. Simultaneous approximation needs a quantitative dimension calculation. FN permits constants below random and substantial subpower suppression.

The actual E1 factor-base size range is 5–39; 8–36 describes the post-hoc subset. P1c’s directional pass does not confirm its registered small-order-character explanation: the observed characters have large order.

For E2, BOX proves a lower bound from ell. It does not universally prove ell is worst. I independently recomputed ell’s first hit in all 20 uncensored cases: it equals the recorded m* in every case. That is finite-data confirmation beyond the published post-hoc lower-bound comparison. The ten censored cases do not establish the exact worst target.

Censored values mean “>cap,” not infinity. P2f still fails using the available lower bounds. The seven adverse pairs are seven pairs in a seeded balanced-semiprime scan, not an asymptotic frequency for uniform admissible moduli.

The raw Gauss/canonical comparison also changes b and density. Its whole factor-of-100 difference cannot be assigned to inherited vectors without matched-parameter controls.

10. Literature: OVERCLAIMED and materially incomplete.

Directly relevant missing prior art includes:

- [Hardy–Xu, Helson’s conjecture for smooth numbers, 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.03430), concerning first absolute moments of Steinhaus sums over smooth numbers.
- [Hardy–Xu, Character sums over smooth numbers, July 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.00592), studying precisely the first absolute character moment equal to the canonical multiplicative algebra norm.

The latter’s useful saving requires a modulus sufficiently larger than the summation range. It neither proves FN’s lower bound at x=N−1 nor contradicts its coarse exponent. It does undermine the impression that smooth-character L1 behavior is unexplored or that sub-random norms must be artifacts.

The references there to Harper, Drappeau–Granville–Shao, and Shparlinski also belong in the discussion. Distinguish multiplicative-character L1 norms, additive exponential-sum norms, and character sup norms.

I found no verified prior statement of FN’s exact composite-modulus lower bound or the precise BOX-infinity theorem. That does not certify novelty. FN’s duality is elementary; BOX-infinity combines classical sieve estimates with an old small-offset-modulus congruence idea. [Solinas’s generalized Mersenne work](https://cacr.uwaterloo.ca/techreports/1999/corr99-39.pdf) is background for that idea, not a prior proof of BOX-infinity. PT is an elementary padding reduction.

Ankeny/Bach concern subgroup escape and generation. They do not provide a general theorem that every smooth-number certificate requires small Fourier-algebra norm. [Bach–Sorenson’s random factored smooth-integer generation](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.07445) is relevant background but does not solve prescribed residues.

The prime-finding analogy is reasonable, with the task specified as uniformly finding a prime in [N,2N]. Polymath’s parity result is not a prime-finding algorithm breaking the square-root bound. Present-day best-known claims need more than a 2012 abstract. [Polymath paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1009.3956).

Finally, “deterministic subexponential relation collection in F_p* is unknown” is false without a definition: arbitrary full-Q-rank relations are trivial—output (p−1)e_q. Deterministic sieving of candidate relations is also familiar, generally without the required uniform proved yield/rank bound. The substantive unestablished task is short prescribed-target DSR witnesses or sufficiently informative/saturated index-calculus relations. An unsaturated full-rank lattice does not determine discrete logarithms.

Required fixes, ordered by severity:

FATAL:
- Replace SC’s false equivalence by the height-coupled statement.
- Withdraw the proved blanket impossibility claim for generic derandomizers.
- Withdraw TH-wrap’s status as the exact weakest obstacle; formulate a precise restricted sufficient route.

MAJOR:
- Repair FN’s nonunit translate and Gauss-density proof; reconcile the minorant contradiction.
- Correct SB’s density quantifier and unsupported finite-group factor 7.
- Correct PT’s running-time accounting and Pollard–Strassen exponent; remove separation rhetoric.
- Separate AA’s valid theorem from coarse sharpness and §2.7’s one-hit/all-target confusion.
- Add first-moment prior art and define the intended relation-collection task.
- Downgrade E1’s causal diagnosis and separate E2’s theorem, observations, and censoring.

MINOR:
- Separate B_* from B_N and spell out the valid sieve hypotheses in BOX-infinity.
- State RW’s mixing/density ranges, exact sampling convention, and distinct endpoint heights.
- Keep probability assessments and literature-search outcomes explicitly subjective or limited.